window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.4","title":"Sample Proportions","goal":"Simulate thousands of polls to see that the spread of the sample proportion matches the formula square root of p(1-p)/n.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"One poll","text":"The lesson's poll: the true support for a bill is p = 0.54 and we ask n = 1,500 voters. Each voter says yes with probability p, so the number of yes answers is a binomial draw.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(2024)\np, n = 0.54, 1500\n\nyes = rng.binomial(n, p)\nprint(f'yes answers: {yes} of {n}')\nprint(f'sample proportion p-hat = {yes / n:.4f}')","out":"yes answers: 781 of 1500\nsample proportion p-hat = 0.5207","figs":[]},{"title":"Ten thousand polls","text":"Now run the same poll 10,000 times. Each p-hat differs a little because of sampling variability. Their average should be near p, and their SD should be near the formula.","code":"p_hats = rng.binomial(n, p, size=10_000) / n\n\nprint(f'mean of p-hats          = {p_hats.mean():.4f}')\nprint(f'SD of p-hats            = {p_hats.std(ddof=1):.4f}')\nprint(f'sqrt(p(1-p)/n) formula  = {np.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n):.4f}')","out":"mean of p-hats          = 0.5400\nSD of p-hats            = 0.0130\nsqrt(p(1-p)/n) formula  = 0.0129","figs":[]},{"title":"Small samples wobble more","text":"The lesson's three cases. The last column checks the rule that n times p and n times (1 minus p) should both be at least 10 for the normal model to fit well.","code":"print('   n     p   SD of p-hats   formula   both n*p, n*(1-p) >= 10?')\nfor n, p in ((20, 0.35), (200, 0.40), (1500, 0.54)):\n    ph = rng.binomial(n, p, size=10_000) / n\n    ok = n * p >= 10 and n * (1 - p) >= 10\n    print(f'{n:5d}  {p:.2f}   {ph.std(ddof=1):11.4f}   {np.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n):7.4f}   {ok}')","out":"   n     p   SD of p-hats   formula   both n*p, n*(1-p) >= 10?\n   20  0.35        0.1053    0.1067   False\n  200  0.40        0.0345    0.0346   True\n 1500  0.54        0.0129    0.0129   True","figs":[]},{"title":"The margin of error","text":"A 95% margin of error is about 1.96 standard errors. If that is right, about 95% of polls should land within that distance of the true p.","code":"n, p = 1500, 0.54\nmoe = 1.96 * np.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)\np_hats = rng.binomial(n, p, size=10_000) / n\ninside = (np.abs(p_hats - p) <= moe).mean()\n\nprint(f'margin of error = {moe:.4f}')\nprint(f'share of polls within it = {inside:.3f}')","out":"margin of error = 0.0252\nshare of polls within it = 0.951","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"set.seed(2024)\np <- 0.54; n <- 1500\np_hats <- rbinom(10000, n, p) / n\nmean(p_hats)\nsd(p_hats)\nsqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)","expect":"mean about 0.54; sd about 0.0129; formula 0.01287 (Python: 0.5400, 0.0130, 0.0129)","note":"rbinom(10000, n, p) draws 10,000 polls at once. Simulated values are close to Python's but not identical; the formula value 0.0129 is exact."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (RV.BINOM), then Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives","syntax":"SET SEED=2024.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP poll = 1 TO 10000.\nCOMPUTE phat = RV.BINOM(1500, 0.54) / 1500.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=phat /STATISTICS=MEAN STDDEV.","note":"This simulates 10,000 polls; compare its Std. Deviation with the formula SQRT(0.54*0.46/1500) = 0.0129. Simulated results are close to Python's, not identical."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 2024\nset obs 10000\ngenerate phat = rbinomial(1500, 0.54) / 1500\nsummarize phat\ndisplay sqrt(0.54 * 0.46 / 1500)","note":"The Mean and Std. dev. of phat should be near 0.54 and 0.0129; simulated numbers are close to Python's but not identical."},"try":["Change p to 0.50 and predict whether the SD of the p-hats goes up or down.","Change n to 6000 and predict the new SD (it should be half of the n = 1500 value).","Try n = 20 with p = 0.02 and notice that many polls give exactly 0. Is the bell shape still good?"]});
